WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.680
- I definitely believe
cooking is my blood.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.340
I feel like cooking chose me.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.880
Food is life.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.240
Food is everything.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.020
Hi, my name is Ayo Cherry,

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.780
and I am a private chef.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.580
I started cooking when I was eight

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.170
and luckily I got to work
at my aunt's restaurant.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.440
And then my grandmother
is a phenomenal cook.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.950
She taught me literally everything I know.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.720
My private chef journey
started a little crazy,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.480
I began working with a personal trainer

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.830
who was preparing meals for his clients

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.890
and he asked me, would I like to prepare

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.030
the meals for his clients.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:38.020
And from there, I got a lot
of other personal trainers

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.180
and I was preparing food for everyone.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.830
And the chef friend of mine asked me

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.450
if I would like to cook
for Lil Wayne one day.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.560
I went and I tried out

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.470
and he liked the food, luckily.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.590
I made a well done steak and
some potato chips. (laughing)

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:57.590
And all of a sudden I was a
private chef to a celebrity.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.610
Having your own business feels a lot like

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.740
what I hear people with
children say about parenting.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.250
It's rough, there are days I hate it,

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.620
I don't know what I'm
doing, but you love it

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.210
and you get up and you just keep doing it.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.800
And then eventually
like good things happen.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.920
My workspace is peoples' homes.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.530
Cooking in someone else's kitchen

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.760
is a lot of trial and error

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.850
because no one has as many things as I do.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:28.000
It's a lot of adjusting to the situation.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.700
The first time we go somewhere,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.060
I bring everything I have
because you just never know.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.400
My kitchen is organized by my things,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.470
my clients things, I have more spoons

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.880
than any one individual should need.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.650
I have a lot of spatulas.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.970
I just have multiples of a lot of things.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.200
And I basically set up everything I need

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.160
for meal prep in one area.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.850
And I section off my things
about spices, sweet and savory

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.550
to refrigerators, and quite frankly,

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.240
I need a third and maybe a deep freezer.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.020
A typical week for me starts on Friday.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.390
Friday's admin day,

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.760
we do all the invoices
from the week before,

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.020
we create menus, we write prep lists

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.230
and we really get ready
to move into the week.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.580
Saturday is a free day for me,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.680
I try to keep one solid day for myself.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.970
Sundays I go grocery shopping,

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.770
I buy everything to get me through Monday

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.637
and ideally in a perfect world Tuesday

00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:24.860
On Mondays, I wake up around 4:00 AM.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:28.080
I prepare vegan meal prep
for two separate clients

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.590
and that's 10 meals each.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.020
I drop one of them off,

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.230
I drop the second one off.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:33.870
And then I go to the grocery store

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:37.880
to get anything extra that
I need from my clients

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.870
that I cook for after
my drop-offs on Monday.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.080
Tuesday, I do the same thing.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.470
I cook for a family of four.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.780
I wake up, I go to grocery
store, I cook for them.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.780
Wednesdays I wake up, I eat,
I drive an hour from my house.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.480
I stopped at the grocery stores

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.840
and any market for whatever along the way.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.310
And then I cook for a family of three,

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.297
dinner and then I meal
prep for them for them

00:03:01.297 --> 00:03:02.830
for the following day.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.250
On Thursdays, I go back to
my same clients from Monday

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.790
and I complete the rest of
their week's worth of meals.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.520
Then I go home and I pass out

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.950
because that is the end
of the week. (laughing)

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.000
It is a lot.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.120
Personal time is difficult to work out.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.740
My friends are always fussing at me,

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:21.573
like, what are you doing?

00:03:21.573 --> 00:03:22.740
I'm like, I'm making a menu.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.480
If I have a long car ride somewhere,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.850
I'm going to find some
time to write something,

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:29.480
send a text, work in some sort of way.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.810
The door of clients rotates
a lot in the beginning

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.060
because you're trying to figure them out,

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.300
they're trying to figure you out.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.000
Sometimes your culinary style

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.230
just simply doesn't match for someone.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.380
And it's a lot like
being in relationships.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.820
Sometimes they're just not your people.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.290
Catering for events is my time to shine.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.290
We put on like this
display for their families.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:55.050
It's always so pretty
and it's always so extra.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.720
I put like gold leaves on the turkey,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.740
like there's usually a lot going on

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.860
but I always have this thing
with my clients where I'm like,

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.370
like, are we showing out or
are we just keeping it cool?

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.531
Because that lets me know like what level

00:04:08.531 --> 00:04:10.600
of event we're having.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.330
This most recent
Thanksgiving for my clients

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:14.980
I made Southern food.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.640
They had family in from the UK

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:19.830
and they don't really have
Southern food there obviously.

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:22.410
So I made a huge Southern feast,

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.600
there was a baked mac and
cheese because of the,

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.780
there was air fried chicken.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.610
I know from on Southerners
are rolling your eyes,

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.340
you'll be fine, it's L.A.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:31.940
It's air fried chicken,

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.300
I did some air fried Turkey
wings, and legs and breasts.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.340
And you know, just broke
down the whole turkey.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:39.950
And did collard greens and green beans.

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.810
And, you know, just
mashed, sweet potatoes,

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:44.970
mashed potatoes, just all the classics.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:48.800
And we also added a rack
of lamb to Thanksgiving,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.630
which isn't typically a
Southern classic, but it's good.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.100
And Thanksgiving is all about what's good.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.330
I feel like I care on an almost

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.180
maternal level about my clients.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.770
I like to add touches that I
know will excite my clients.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.240
Something else I like to do for clients

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.230
is make sure it looks really appetizing

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:11.346
because you eat with your eyes first

00:05:11.346 --> 00:05:13.810
and meal prep can get really stale.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:17.400
So I really go out of my way
to make a fully composed dish

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.710
and make sure it's pretty,

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:19.680
and there are colors

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.400
and use some like really good
knife cuts and give them,

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.470
you know, some stuff's sliced on a bias

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:25.860
or some really pretty peppers,

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.760
instead of just green peppers, you know,

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.400
just going the extra mile so
it looks visually appealing.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.600
You are feeding people's families.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.230
It's not a small task.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:37.930
And I do take a lot of pride

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:39.640
in the amount of knowledge I have

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.740
to make sure this food is like nourishing,

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.340
is correctly prepared, it tastes good.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.790
You know, food is something
that brings us all together

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:50.570
and you are literally responsible
for creating that moment

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:52.360
for your clients every single day.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.430
When I labeled the meal for meal prep,

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:55.430
I leave them instruction

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.850
on what I think would be
the best way to reheat it.

00:05:57.850 --> 00:05:59.590
It does really help to know like,

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:00.560
hey, we have an air fryer,

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.860
if we put half of this
meal in the air fryer,

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.340
we can put some of it in the oven

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.670
and it'll get as close to
the texture and freshness

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:09.684
as it was when it was prepared.

00:06:09.684 --> 00:06:14.440
It is difficult when you are
a private chef to say, no.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.190
It is difficult to say
no to opportunities.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:18.470
It is difficult to say no,

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.938
especially when you're in
someone's home and in their face.

00:06:20.938 --> 00:06:21.771
(laughing)

00:06:21.771 --> 00:06:25.750
It is hard to go from haha
hehe, here's some rose,

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:30.600
here's your food, no you
can't call another 10 people.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.290
It is a difficult thing
to set those boundaries.

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:37.050
It is difficult to make sure
I have time for me time.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:38.690
I'm slightly getting better at it

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:41.330
but it is a very difficult thing to say,

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:45.450
hey, I'm not going to work.
I'm going to do nothing.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:46.800
That is a hard thing to do

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.630
without feeling super guilty about it.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.600
So being a private chef always has

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.110
a multitude of opportunities,

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.490
you know, you're not just in
people's homes doing meal prep.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.660
You could be traveling with a client,

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:00.150
I've flown on my client's private jet

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:01.810
with them to Australia.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.470
You could also be working,
you know, overseas for people.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:07.980
There's a lot of different opportunities

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.010
in the private chef world.

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:13.260
I physically don't think
I could do this forever.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.660
The hours are long, you know,

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:16.290
you're carrying a lot of stuff.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:19.640
So after this, I think
I could do a cookbook

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.440
or I can have a food truck.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:23.840
I have a lot of ideas in my head

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.690
but I'm pretty much open to whatever

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:27.500
the universe has for me.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:29.960
My advice to any young chefs

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:32.370
who are interested in the private sector

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.690
is talk to other private chefs.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:37.310
And when I moved to Los Angeles,

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:39.366
I leaned on for many chefs here.

00:07:39.366 --> 00:07:42.970
And the amount of help
you get from other people

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:45.000
in your sector is invaluable.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.620
Even before a private chefing,

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.890
I think starting in a
restaurant teaches you

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:52.070
so many of those basic skills you need

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.020
that will help you through anything,

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:55.670
no matter what you end up doing.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:57.600
So if you love food, I say, go for it.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.710
I mean, you're creating
happy moments for people.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:01.483
There's nothing better than that.

00:08:01.483 --> 00:08:04.150
(upbeat music)

